**Corollary 5.2 (Explicitly restricted vanishing-sequence statement).** Suppose a sequence of original observers has all reachable p and suspended emissions on the designated fibre in $[a,b]$, and all its reachable suspended alpha-successor averages there are native. If the installed prior has a supported nonendpoint, their common conf/conf excesses are bounded below by (5.3). Thus no such regular residual-native sequence vanishes. No corresponding gap or sequence theorem is claimed when arbitrary zero/unit or out-of-interval emissions are allowed: clipping can change $J_y$ and need not preserve residual-native membership.

Proof. Extract the unchanged common support of each observer. The two hypotheses pass to its reachable positive-row subset, and its table risks are dominated by the original configuration risks. Apply Theorem 5.1. $\square$

